.TH ATLAS 4
.SH NAME
atlas \- the desktop: screen, windows, terminals (port "atlas")
.SH SYNOPSIS
Port
.B atlas
(its terminals are channels on it, "atlas#N").
.B /bin/atlas
is started at boot by
.BR init (8),
with device rights (the screen); with no display card it ends with status 0
and is not started again.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B atlas
is the SIEOS desktop, laid out as BeOS was.
One process does it all, to keep the system small: it drives the screen,
draws, shows the login screen and the first start, is the terminal of the
shells in its windows, edits files, lists folders, keeps the settings and
talks to the assistant.
It takes the keyboard and mouse from the console
.RB ( CON_INPUT ,
.BR con (4)).
.PP
Each terminal window is a console for the shell in it: the shell's identity
names "atlas#N", and its CON_WRITE and CON_READ come to atlas on that
channel, with the same line editing as the text console.
Apps are started from launchers,
.BR app (5).
Nothing runs when nothing happens: the picture changes only on an event, and
only the changed region is sent to the screen.
.PP
Other programs may open windows of their own
.RB ( <mk/atlas.h> ):
they draw the insides into shared memory, in 32-bit 0x00RRGGBB pixels, and
atlas frames them; at most 8 at once; only the user logged in, or root.
.SH MESSAGES
.TP
.B ATLAS_WIN_OPEN
sbuf = the title \-> a window id; its first event is ATW_SIZE.
.TP
.B ATLAS_WIN_BUFFER
w[1] = window, w[2] = a shared memory object
.RB ( sys_shm_create (2))
with its pixels, w[3] = width | height << 16 \-> 0.
.TP
.B ATLAS_WIN_DAMAGE
w[1] = window, sbuf = atlas_rect_t[]: what changed \-> 0 once it is on the
screen (pace frames by it).
.TP
.B ATLAS_WIN_EVENT
w[1] = window; rbuf <\- atlas_event_t[] (ATW_KEY, ATW_POINTER, ATW_SIZE,
ATW_FOCUS, ATW_CLOSE); waits for input \-> how many.
.TP
.BR ATLAS_WIN_TITLE ", " ATLAS_WIN_CLOSE
a new title; close the window.
.TP
.BR ATLAS_WIN_CLIP_GET ", " ATLAS_WIN_CLIP_SET
the clipboard's text (at most 16 KiB).
.TP
.B ATLAS_NOTE
(root: btd) sbuf = a line to show for a while; w[1] = 1: a question, with
Accept and Decline.
.PP
Errors: \-EPERM (not the user's desktop), \-EBADF (no such window),
\-EMFILE (too many).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/apps/*.app
everyone's launchers;
.B ~/*.app
the user's own
.TP
.B /etc/display.conf
the screen size, the panel's brightness, what the lid does
.TP
.BR ~/.look ", " ~/.mouse
the user's look and size, and mouse settings
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR app (5),
.BR display.conf (5),
.BR con (4),
.BR siad (4)
